# Reading state variables at a step: the value a slot holds is the most recent word the
# transaction recorded for it, so `print counter` moves with the `SSTORE`s, and a slot
# nothing has touched is reported as unknown rather than as zero. The counter's absolute
# value depends on how many increments this deployment has seen, so what is pinned here is
# how it changes.
# REQUIRES: soldb
# RUN: printf 'break TestContract.sol:30\ncontinue\nvars\nprint counter\nstorage\nbreak storage 0\ncontinue\nnexti\nprint counter\nquit\n' | %soldb trace %{test_tx} --ethdebug-dir %{contract_address}:TestContract:%{ethdebug_dir} --rpc %{rpc_url} -i 2>&1 | FileCheck %s

# `increment` has already added its amount to the counter by line 30.
# CHECK: Breakpoint #1 hit at step {{[0-9]+}}, TestContract.sol:30
# CHECK: State:
# CHECK-NEXT: uint256 counter = [[#COUNT:]] [slot 0x0]
# CHECK-NEXT: mapping(address => uint256) balances = <mapping; index it with [key]> [slot 0x1]
# CHECK: uint256 counter = [[#COUNT]] [slot 0x0]
# The storage view is every slot known at this step, not only the one this opcode touched.
# CHECK: Storage: {{.*}}0x{{[0-9a-f]+}}
# CHECK-NEXT: 0x0 = 0x{{[0-9a-f]+}}
# `increment2` adds the same amount again; the write takes effect after its `SSTORE`.
# CHECK: Breakpoint #2 hit at step {{[0-9]+}}, storage slot 0x0
# CHECK: uint256 counter = [[#COUNT+4]] [slot 0x0]

# A mapping entry is read by key. The transaction never touched this one, so its value
# comes from the node, at the block before the transaction's, and says so.
# RUN: printf 'print balances\nprint counter\nprint balances[0xf39Fd6e51aad88F6F4ce6aB8827279cffFb92266]\nquit\n' | %soldb trace %{test_tx} --ethdebug-dir %{contract_address}:TestContract:%{ethdebug_dir} --rpc %{rpc_url} -i 2>&1 | FileCheck --check-prefix=MAPPING %s

# MAPPING: mapping(address => uint256) balances = <mapping; index it with [key]> [slot 0x1]
# The counter before the transaction ran, plus what `increment` adds, is what the state
# view shows once the first `SSTORE` has happened.
# MAPPING: uint256 counter = [[#BEFORE:]] [slot 0x0, from the chain at block {{[0-9]+}}, before this transaction's block]
# MAPPING: uint256 balances[0xf39Fd6e51aad88F6F4ce6aB8827279cffFb92266] = {{[0-9]+}} [slot 0x{{[0-9a-f]+}}, from the chain at block {{[0-9]+}}, before this transaction's block]

# Without a node there is no chain to read: `soldb run` says the slot is untouched rather
# than reaching for a value.
# RUN: %soldb compile %S/../examples/StateVars.sol -o %t.out --solc %{solc_path} > /dev/null 2>&1
# RUN: printf 'print counter\nquit\n' | %soldb run %t.out/StateVars.bin "touchAll(address)" 0xf39Fd6e51aad88F6F4ce6aB8827279cffFb92266 --ethdebug-dir 0x5FbDB2315678afecB367f032d93F642f64180aa3:StateVars:%t.out -i 2>&1 | FileCheck --check-prefix=NOCHAIN %s

# NOCHAIN: uint256 counter = <unknown: slot 0x0 has not been read or written yet> [slot 0x0]

# A breakpoint can carry a condition over the values the debugger can read: state
# variables, the frame's arguments, and facts about the step. It is checked only where the
# target already matched.
# RUN: printf 'break TestContract.sol:44 if counter > 100000\ncontinue\nquit\n' | %soldb trace %{test_tx} --ethdebug-dir %{contract_address}:TestContract:%{ethdebug_dir} --rpc %{rpc_url} -i 2>&1 | FileCheck --check-prefix=NEVER %s

# NEVER: Breakpoint #1 set at TestContract.sol:44 if counter > 100000
# NEVER: End of trace at step {{[0-9]+}}
# NEVER-NOT: Breakpoint #1 hit

# RUN: printf 'break increment2 if amount == 4\ncontinue\nbt\nbreak TestContract.sol:44 if counter >= 4\ncontinue\nprint counter\nquit\n' | %soldb trace %{test_tx} --ethdebug-dir %{contract_address}:TestContract:%{ethdebug_dir} --rpc %{rpc_url} -i 2>&1 | FileCheck --check-prefix=WHEN %s

# WHEN: Breakpoint #1 set at function TestContract.increment2 at TestContract.sol:39 if amount == 4
# WHEN: Breakpoint #1 hit at step {{[0-9]+}}, function TestContract.increment2 at TestContract.sol:39 if amount == 4
# WHEN: #0  increment2(amount = 4) at TestContract.sol:39
# WHEN: Breakpoint #2 hit at step {{[0-9]+}}, TestContract.sol:44 if counter >= 4

# A condition that cannot be read does not stop, and says why instead of failing silently.
# RUN: printf 'break TestContract.sol:44 if nosuch > 1\ncontinue\nquit\n' | %soldb trace %{test_tx} --ethdebug-dir %{contract_address}:TestContract:%{ethdebug_dir} --rpc %{rpc_url} -i 2>&1 | FileCheck --check-prefix=UNREADABLE %s

# UNREADABLE: A breakpoint condition never held: `nosuch > 1` could not be evaluated: no state variable named `nosuch` in the storage layout
